{"verdict":"incorrect","findings":[{"id":"reviewer#1","title":"Clear and notify inflight state when computation fails","comment":"`_inflight[key]` is removed and its event is set only after `factory()` returns successfully. If the factory raises or is cancelled, the unset event remains forever; the same job's next retry and every later caller for that key block at `pending.wait()`, so a transient handler failure can hang workers and prevent `drain()` from completing. Put inflight cleanup and waiter notification in a `finally` path, with a way for joiners to observe the failed computation.","priority":"P1","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.XmiWTC/cache.py","lines":"127-133","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#2","title":"Release the global guard before awaiting per-key work","comment":"The `_guard` context now spans both `lock.acquire()` and the caller's entire `yield` block. While any key is held, no coroutine can even look up a different key, collapsing the worker pool to one active cache computation; a contending `hold(..., timeout=...)` also waits outside the timed `wait_for`, so its timeout cannot fire while the current holder is running. Keep the guard only around map/refcount mutations, then acquire and hold the per-key lock after releasing it.","priority":"P1","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.XmiWTC/keylock.py","lines":"34-47","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#3","title":"Check expiry before extending the deadline","comment":"The lookup increments `hits` and slides `expires_at` before testing whether the old deadline passed. Once an entry has reached `refresh_after_hits`, even a read hours after its TTL refreshes the deadline first and returns the stale value; for example, an entry with two prior hits and a threshold of three survives its first post-expiry read. Test and remove expired entries before applying hit-based refreshes so the documented TTL remains effective.","priority":"P1","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.XmiWTC/cache.py","lines":"74-80","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#4","title":"Apply the requested timeout while joining a computation","comment":"When another computation is already inflight, this unconditional event wait happens before either the per-call `timeout` or configured `_lock_timeout` is applied. Under same-key contention, a caller requesting a short timeout will therefore wait for the full duration of a slow or stuck factory instead of raising `asyncio.TimeoutError`; wrap the join wait in the same timeout budget or represent the inflight computation with a timed awaitable.","priority":"P1","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.XmiWTC/cache.py","lines":"105-111","sources":["reviewer"]},{"id":"reviewer#5","title":"Read the renamed cache counters when computing hit rate","comment":"The cache now increments `cache.get.hit`, `cache.get.hit_after_wait`, and `cache.get.miss`, but this method still reads the old names. Consequently `total` remains zero, the reported hit rate is always `1.0`, and `warm_stale_keys()` always returns without warming in every queue using these counters. Update the metric names here to match the producers.","priority":"P2","file":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T/review-bench.XmiWTC/jobqueue.py","lines":"202-205","sources":["reviewer"]}],"lenses":[],"target":{"base":"main","baseCommit":"76aa408900f70d839f5cdba514c5890e1e3a847d","headCommit":"ba5e24080ea111abaff68ad5a46559479bc3624f","repo":"/var/folders/c1/l4z5k02n2779byvnymzxsvth0000gn/T//review-bench.XmiWTC"},"level":"medium","coverage":[{"finder":"reviewer","lens":null,"status":"ok","candidates":5}],"explanation":"incorrect — The new inflight and locking paths can hang retries, ignore timeouts, and serialize unrelated keys, while the sliding-TTL ordering can serve expired values indefinitely. The renamed metrics also disable the warm-up feature and misreport cache performance."}
